====
\f0\b DAY-3
\f1\b0 ======================================================================================================================================================	 \
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 What is a Router?\
     \

\f1\b0 the fundamental role of a 
\f0\b Router
\f1\b0  in networking and how it differs from a 
\f5\i switch
\f1\i0 .\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \

\f0\b 1. Switch vs. Router
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Switch (Layer 2):
\f1\b0 \'a0Connects devices within the same network. It uses\'a0
\f0\b MAC addresses
\f1\b0 \'a0to send data (frames) to the correct local device .  \
\ls9\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}      	
\f0\b\fs26 Router (Layer 3):
\f1\b0 \'a0Connects different, separate networks. It uses\'a0
\f0\b IP addresses
\f1\b0 \'a0(packets) to determine how to move data between one network and another .\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 2. Why do we need Routers?
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 Devices on the same network communicate directly via their\'a0
\f5\i switch
\f1\i0 \'a0using\'a0
\f5\i ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)
\f1\i0 \'a0to map IP addresses to MAC addresses .\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 When a device tries to send data to an IP address outside its local range, it needs a\'a0
\f0\b Default Gateway
\f1\b0 \'a0(the router). Without a router, communication between different networks is impossible .\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 3. How a Router Works
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Routing Table:
\f1\b0 \'a0The router maintains a "map" of different networks and knows exactly which interface to use to reach them .\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 The Process:
\f1\b0 \'a0When a router receives a packet from one network, it checks its routing table (Layer 3) to find the destination network.hen uses ARP (Layer 2) to find the MAC address of the device on the destination network to deliver the frame .\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 4. Key Concepts to learn 
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
