===DAY-5=======================================================================================================================================================\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 explaining the history and function of networking models like TCP/IP and OSI, which allow different devices from different manufacturers to communicate with one another .\
\

\f0\b Key Concepts
\f1\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 1. Why We Need Networking Models
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 In the early days of computing, networks were\'a0
\f0\b proprietary
\f1\b0 , meaning devices from one company (like IBM) could not talk to devices from another .\
\ls21\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 Networking models act as a set of standard rules that all manufacturers agree to use so that different systems can connect and share data .\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 2. The TCP/IP Model (The Industry Standard)
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 This is the model actually used by modern computers to communicate over the internet .\
\pard\pardeftab560\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs18 \cf0 	\'95		\'95	
\f1\fs26 It organizes network functions into layers to make complex systems easier to understand and manage . For example, the\'a0
\f0\b Physical layer
\f1\b0 \'a0deals with cables and electricity, while the\'a0
\f0\b Network layer
\f1\b0 \'a0uses IP addresses and routers \
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 3. The OSI Model (The Reference Framework)
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 While the TCP/IP model won the 'war' of standards, the\'a0
\f0\b OSI (Open Systems Interconnect) model
\f1\b0 \'a0is still the primary language used by engineers to describe network issues .\
\ls23\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 It has seven layers compared to the TCP/IP model's five. Engineers often use terms from the OSI model (e.g., 'Layer 7 application issue') when troubleshooting .\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Study Advises for Beginners;\

\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Memorization Tip:
\f1\b0 \'a0Use mnemonic devices to remember the seven layers of the OSI model. For top-down, use:\'a0
\f0\b 'All People Seem To Need Data Processing'
\f1\b0 \'a0. For bottom-up, use:\'a0
\f0\b 'Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away'
\f1\b0 \'a0.\
\ls24\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Practical Application:
\f1\b0 \'a0When troubleshooting, identify which 'layer' the problem is occurring in. This helps narrow down whether it is a physical cable issue (Layer 1), a switching issue (Layer 2), or an application issue (Layer 7).\
\pard\pardeftab560\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
