\f0\b OSI Model Upper Layers and Transport:\

\f1\b0 \
This focuses on layers 4 through 7 of the OSI model, explaining how your computer communicates with services like 
\f5\i YouTube
\f1\i0 .\
\

\f0\b 1. The Upper Layers (Application, Presentation, Session)
\f1\b0  \
\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls33\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Application Layer (Layer 7):
\f1\b0 \'a0The interface for programs (like web browsers) to access network services .\
\ls33\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Presentation Layer (Layer 6):
\f1\b0 \'a0Handles data formatting (e.g., HTML, JPEG, PDF) and encryption, such as\'a0
\f5\i SSL/TLS
\f1\i0 \'a0.\
\ls33\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Session Layer (Layer 5):
\f1\b0 \'a0Manages, maintains, and tears down the communication conversation between hosts . Examples include\'a0
\f5\i VPNs (L2TP)
\f1\i0 \'a0and\'a0
\f5\i SOCKS proxies
\f1\i0 .\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 2. The Transport Layer (Layer 4)
\f1\b0  \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls34\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 TCP (Transmission Control Protocol):
\f1\b0 \'a0A reliable, "naggy" protocol that uses a\'a0
\f0\b three-way handshake
\f1\b0 \'a0(SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) to ensure data is received accurately .\
\ls34\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 UDP (User Datagram Protocol):
\f1\b0 \'a0A fast protocol that does not wait for verification. Ideal for real-time traffic like live video streaming or gaming where minor packet loss is preferable to latency .\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 3. Ports (Layer 4 Addressing)
\f1\b0  \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls35\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Well-Known Ports:
\f1\b0 \'a0Reserved (0-1023) to identify services (e.g.,\'a0
\f0\b 443
\f1\b0 \'a0for\'a0
\f5\i HTTPS
\f1\i0 ,\'a0
\f0\b 22
\f1\b0 \'a0for\'a0
\f5\i SSH
\f1\i0 ,\'a0
\f0\b 21
\f1\b0 \'a0for\'a0
\f5\i FTP
\f1\i0 ) .\
\ls35\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Ephemeral Ports:
\f1\b0 \'a0Temporary source ports chosen by the client to keep track of multiple active connections simultaneously .\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
Advice for Your CCNA Journey\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls36\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	1.	}
\f0\b Don't memorize, understand the flow:
\f1\b0 \'a0Instead of just memorizing port numbers, focus on the\'a0
\f5\i process
\f1\i0 \'a0of encapsulation and de-encapsulation. Understanding\'a0
\f5\i why
\f1\i0 \'a0a protocol chooses TCP over UDP is more valuable than rote memorization for the exam.\
{\listtext	2.	}
\f0\b Use Lab Software:
