\f0\b Notes: Data Center Network Architecture
\f1\b0 \
This video covers the evolution of data center network designs, shifting from traditional models to modern standards required for today's high-speed, virtualized environments.\

\f0\b 1. What is a Data Center? 
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls43\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 Data centers house the servers, routers, and switches that power internet resources.\
\ls43\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 They range from small, private server closets to massive, hyperscale facilities (like those used by\'a0
\f5\i Google
\f1\i0 ,\'a0
\f5\i Facebook
\f1\i0 , or\'a0
\f5\i AWS
\f1\i0 ).\
\ls43\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 Companies may manage their own equipment or rent space/cloud services in a larger facility to ensure reliable power and cooling.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 2. The Traditional Three-Tier Design 
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls44\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Hierarchy:
\f1\b0 \'a0Access Layer (Top of Rack switches) -> Distribution Layer -> Core Layer.\
\ls44\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Focus:
\f1\b0 \'a0Designed primarily for\'a0
\f0\b North-South traffic
\f1\b0 \'a0(data moving between the internet and internal servers).\
\ls44\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Downfalls:
\f1\b0 \'a0It struggles with modern\'a0
\f0\b East-West traffic
\f1\b0 \'a0(server-to-server communication). Because of virtualization, 80% of current traffic is East-West, and the old design forced inefficient, high-hop paths. Additionally,\'a0
\f5\i Spanning Tree Protocol
\f1\i0 \'a0limits performance by blocking redundant links to prevent loops.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 3. The Modern Spine-Leaf Design 
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls45\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Architecture:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls45\ilvl1
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Leaf Switches:
\f1\b0 \'a0Connect to the servers (formerly Access Layer).\
\ls45\ilvl1
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Spine Switches:
\f1\b0 \'a0The high-speed backbone (replaces Distribution/Core layers).\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls45\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Connectivity:
\f1\b0 \'a0Every Leaf switch connects to every Spine switch, creating a\'a0
\f0\b full mesh
\f1\b0 \'a0topology.\
\ls45\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Advantages:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls45\ilvl1
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Predictable Performance:
\f1\b0 \'a0Any server-to-server communication is a maximum of\'a0
\f0\b two hops
\f1\b0 .\
