In this demonstrates how data travels through a network using the 
\f0\b OSI model
\f1\b0  and the 
\f0\b TCP/IP model
\f1\b0  by tracing a web request from a user (
\f5\i Johnny
\f1\i0 ) to a server website (google.com).\

\f0\b Core Concepts:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls57\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Encapsulation:
\f1\b0 \'a0The process of wrapping data in headers as it moves down the network stack (from Layer 7 to Layer 2).\
\ls57\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 De-encapsulation:
\f1\b0 \'a0The reverse process performed by receiving devices to inspect the data at each layer.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The Layers in Action:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls58\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Application Layer (Layer 7):
\f1\b0 \'a0Initiates the request using the\'a0
\f5\i HTTPS
\f1\i0 \'a0protocol .\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Transport Layer (Layer 4):
\f1\b0 \'a0Adds a header indicating the protocol (
\f5\i TCP
\f1\i0 ) and port (
\f5\i 443
\f1\i0 ). Data at this stage is called a\'a0
\f0\b segment
\f1\b0 \'a0.\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Network Layer (Layer 3):
\f1\b0 \'a0Adds source and destination\'a0
\f5\i IP addresses
\f1\i0 . Data at this stage is called a\'a0
\f0\b packet
\f1\b0 \'a0.\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Data Link Layer (Layer 2):
\f1\b0 \'a0Adds\'a0
\f5\i MAC addresses
\f1\i0 \'a0for switch communication. Data at this stage is called a\'a0
\f0\b frame
\f1\b0 \'a0.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Network Device Behavior:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Switches:
\f1\b0 \'a0Only see Layer 2 (frames) and use the\'a0
\f5\i CAM table
\f1\i0 \'a0to forward data to the correct port .\
\pard\pardeftab560\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs18 \cf0 	\'95.   
\f0\b\fs26 Routers:
\f1\b0 \'a0See Layer 3 (packets) and use the\'a0
\f5\i routing table
\f1\i0 \'a0to determine the path, then re-encapsulate the packet into a new frame with updated\'a0
\f5\i MAC addresses
\f1\i0 \'a0to send it to the next hop .\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \

\f0\b Conclusion:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Upon reaching the destination server, the device strips away each header layer by layer until the original application data is accessed and processed .\
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 here is some 
\f0\b advice for your networking journey
\f1\b0 :                     \
        1.   
\f0\b Use Lab Environments:
\f1\b0  , theory is best understood by 
\f0\b actively practicing
\f1\b0 . Download software like 
\f5\i Packet Tracer
\f1\i0  to visualize how data flows through a network in real-time rather  than just reading about it.\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls60\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	2.	}
\f0\b Master the Process of Elimination:
\f1\b0  When taking certification exams, use the process of elimination to rule out clearly incorrect answers . Even if you don't know the exact answer, you can often identify options that belong to the wrong layers or protocols.\
{\listtext	3.	}
\f0\b Focus on the 'Why':
\f1\b0  Don't just memorize terms. Understanding the 
\f0\b concept of encapsulation
\f1\b0  and how devices like switches (Layer 2) and routers (Layer 3) handle data differently is the foundation of network troubleshooting.\
{\listtext	4.	}
\f0\b Stay Consistent:
\f1\b0  Networking concepts build on each other. The 
\f5\i OSI model
\f1\i0  is your roadmap\'97keep referencing it as you learn about new protocols, as it will help you categorize where differen technologies sit in the stack.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
