\ls61\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}This provides a practical, real-world demonstration of how data traverses a network using the 
\f0\b TCP/IP
\f1\b0  and 
\f0\b OSI networking models
\f1\b0 . The speaker follows a data packet from a laptop to a web server to explain the process of 
\f0\b encapsulation
\f1\b0  and 
\f0\b de-encapsulation
\f1\b0  across seven layers. \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Key Stages of Data Travel:\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Application Layer (Layer 7):
\f1\b0 \'a0The process begins here, where applications (like a web browser) generate data\'97in this case, an HTTPS request to access a website. \
\ls61\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Transport Layer (Layer 4):
\f1\b0 \'a0The computer selects a transport protocol, typically\'a0
\f0\b TCP
\f1\b0 \'a0for reliable delivery or\'a0
\f0\b UDP
\f1\b0 \'a0for speed, and assigns a port (e.g., port 443 for HTTPS). The data is then encapsulated with a header, forming a\'a0
\f0\b segment
\f1\b0 . \
\ls61\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Network Layer (Layer 3):
\f1\b0 \'a0The segment is encapsulated with a header containing\'a0
\f0\b source and destination IP addresses
\f1\b0 , which directs the data through routers. This resulting unit is called a\'a0
\f0\b packet
\f1\b0 . \
\pard\pardeftab560\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}	\'95	
\f0\b\fs26 Data Link Layer (Layer 2):
\f1\b0 \'a0The packet is encapsulated into a\'a0
\f0\b frame
\f1\b0 , which includes a header and trailer containing\'a0
\f0\b MAC addresses
\f1\b0 . This allows the data to move between local devices like switches. \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f7\fs33\fsmilli16800 \cf3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Processing and De-encapsulation:\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls62\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Switch Processing:
\f1\b0 \'a0Switches examine the\'a0
\f0\b frame
\f1\b0 \'a0at the Data Link Layer (Layer 2) using their MAC address table to forward traffic to the correct destination. \
\ls62\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Router Processing:
\f1\b0 \'a0Routers receive the frame, strip the Data Link layer, and look at the\'a0
\f0\b packet
\f1\b0 \'a0at the Network Layer (Layer 3) to determine the routing path. They then re-encapsulate it into a new frame to continue the journey. \
\pard\pardeftab560\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs18 \cf0 	\'95	
\f0\b\fs26 Server Receipt:
\f1\b0 \'a0Upon arrival at the web server, the device performs\'a0
\f0\b de-encapsulation
\f1\b0 , systematically stripping away the headers from the Data Link, Network, and Transport layers to eventually extract the original application data.\
\
